/**
 * @packageDocumentation
 *
 * Service for claiming receiver-claimable UTXOs into an Encrypted Token Account (ETA).
 *
 * @remarks
 * A "receiver-claimable" UTXO is a UTXO that was created by a sender using the **receiver's**
 * X25519 public key.  Only the receiver can supply the matching private key and therefore
 * only the receiver can claim it (contrast with self-claimable UTXOs where the creator
 * can also spend them).
 *
 * **High-level claim pipeline (per batch):**
 *
 * 1. Input validation — batch size (1-4 UTXOs), shared destination address, field-element
 *    bounds for every UTXO field.
 * 2. Generation-index derivation — a claim-specific, privacy-preserving generation index is
 *    derived from the master seed via two chained KMAC256 calls
 *    (`DOMAIN_CLAIM_MODIFIED_GEN_INDEX` then `DOMAIN_CLAIM_EXPANDED_GEN_INDEX`).
 * 3. Transaction Viewing Key (TVK) — generated from the current UTC timestamp so the
 *    transaction can later be correlated by the key holder without linking it on-chain.
 * 4. User commitment — the receiver's Poseidon-based user commitment is reconstructed
 *    from their master viewing key, blinding factors, and Poseidon private key.
 * 5. Per-leaf cryptographic data — nullifiers and random secrets are derived from the
 *    deposit's `modifiedGenerationIndex`.  H1/H2 Poseidon hashes and leaf commitments
 *    are computed for every UTXO in the batch.
 * 6. Linker encryptions — for each leaf, six values (sender address, amount, mint address,
 *    leaf commitment) are encrypted under the TVK using the Poseidon cipher, producing
 *    ciphertexts and keystream commitments that allow compliance auditing.
 * 7. Rescue cipher — seven values (final amount, protocol fees, relayer fees, MVK low/high,
 *    random polynomial factor low/high) are encrypted under the receiver's X25519 key using
 *    the Rescue-prime cipher, giving the MXE network the information it needs to credit the
 *    balance without learning the receiver's identity.
 * 8. Fiat-Shamir challenge — a deterministic Curve25519 field element derived from the
 *    Rescue ciphertexts and commitment; powers up to the fifth are computed and converted
 *    to Base-85 limbs for the circuit.
 * 9. BN254 inverse calculations — limbwise and modular inverses required by the range-check
 *    and consistency sub-circuits inside the Groth16 circuit.
 * 10. Polynomial evaluation — evaluates the fee polynomial at the Fiat-Shamir challenge
 *     point, used by the circuit to prove correct fee deduction without revealing amounts.
 * 11. Public aggregated hash (70 inputs) — a single Poseidon hash over all public inputs
 *     that serves as the sole public input to the Groth16 verifier.
 * 12. ZK proof generation — a Groth16 proof is produced by {@link IZkProverForClaimReceiverClaimableUtxoIntoEncryptedBalance}
 *     for the batch-size-specific circuit.
 * 13. Pre-transactions — (a) optionally close a stale proof PDA, (b) create the proof PDA
 *     with the Rescue ciphertexts and Groth16 proof stored on-chain.
 * 14. Burner transactions — one transaction per pair of UTXOs stores nullifier hashes and
 *     linker data into the `ConfUtxoBurner` PDA so the main claim instruction can reference
 *     them without exceeding account limits.
 * 15. Claim transaction — selects one of four instruction variants
 *     (`anonymously_claim_into_{new,existing}_token_{mxe,shared}_v3`) based on whether the
 *     receiver's ETA already exists and whether it is MXE-only or Shared mode.  The
 *     transaction is partially signed by the ephemeral signer and submitted to the relayer.
 * 16. Relayer submission — all transactions are forwarded through
 *     {@link EncryptedBalanceClaimRelayerForwarderFunction} so they cannot be linked to the
 *     receiver's wallet.
 *
 * **Privacy guarantees:**
 * - The ephemeral signer key is derived deterministically from the master seed and a per-batch
 *   generation offset, meaning it is reproducible for recovery but never reused across claims.
 * - All on-chain transactions are signed by the ephemeral signer or the relayer; the
 *   receiver's main wallet never appears in any transaction.
 * - The Groth16 proof hides which specific UTXO commitments are being spent.
 *
 * @see {@link getReceiverClaimableUtxoToEncryptedBalanceClaimerFunction} — the public factory
 * @see {@link processUtxoInBatch} — the internal per-batch worker
 */
